@@ -0,0 +1,133 @@
+# ProofScope v0.9: Certified drift localization (CMCD)
+
+Base: `entropysherlock/ProofScope` @ `557b3213021165076c1e3f8533389eeacf8c151b`. Lean 4.34.1, core only.
+**LOCAL** marks results from the author's sandbox. **PENDING** marks anything that still needs a GitHub run.
+
+## 0. Mission Zero (infrastructure)
+
+- **Lean download (HTTP 504).** The new `.github/actions/setup-lean` downloads exactly
+  `lean-4.34.1-linux.tar.zst` from the official release with bounded retries (curl retries × 4
+  attempts) and a cache. It verifies **SHA-256 `47bf4bbd…72e4e4`** (computed from a fresh download of
+  the release asset and matching the sandbox copy) and fails closed with `INFRASTRUCTURE_ERROR`
+  (exit 75). It never substitutes another release.
+  - Used by: Engine, ClaimDelta Certified, ClaimDelta Alpha, CMCD.
+  - Tested locally: cached install, `lean --version` check, and tampered-hash rejection.
+- **v0.8 verification and the dossier count (`input_records == dossiers == 1715`).** Both are
+  **PENDING**: GitHub run logs cannot be read from the sandbox (the API returns 403), so ChatGPT must
+  report the v0.8 Research Audit run `42618304840` and the reruns.
+
+## 1. Novelty boundary (papers read 2026-11-09)
+
+| Capability | SemantEq (Li et al., Oct 2026) | ACE / SAE (Yu et al., arXiv 2610.25018) | BEq+ / ProofNetVerif (Poiroux et al. 2025) | BPF (Mohammad & Sheikh, arXiv 2606.16541) | **ProofScope v0.9** |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Certified equivalence | yes (restricted step chain, proof-term audit) | yes (role-preserving alignment) | yes (restricted tactics) | no (score) | yes (uniform entailment over abstracted shared operators) |
+| Certified non-equivalence | no ("Likely") | yes, as "no admissible alignment"; **no countermodel or witness** | no | no (probe "witnesses of drift", LLM-predicted) | **yes: kernel-checked countermodel** |
+| Direction (weaker/stronger) | heuristic | **not reported** | no | not a verdict (signed probes) | **certified** |
+| Both statements true (e.g. `n ≤ 2^n` vs `n < 2^n`) | flags as "Likely" | rejects unrelated provable pairs structurally | accepts 94–96% of unrelated provable pairs (as measured by ACE's authors) | probe-dependent | **certified separation** |
+| Localization of the changed component | no | **not reported** | no | coarse (drift class) | **yes: the edit, with a theorem that its countermodel is global** |
+| Mathlib / real research statements | yes | yes | yes | yes | **no (core Lean only)**: the major gap |
+| Published external benchmark | ProofNetVerif | ProofNetVerif | ProofNetVerif | own splits | **none yet** (PENDING) |
+
+Sources: SemantEq abstract (arXiv/chatpaper, 26 Oct 2026); ACE/SAE full text (arxiv.org/html/2610.25018).
+On ProofNetVerif, ACE reports Noneq on 31.8% of consensus-equivalent pairs, Eq on 0.1% of inequivalent
+pairs, and no directional output. BPF: arXiv 2606.16541 v1 (15 Jun 2026). BEq+ unrelated-provability
+acceptance comes from the ACE paper. These are papers we *read*, not systems we *ran*; except for
+SemantEq (v0.6), no competitor has been executed on our cases.
+
+**The gap.** No surveyed system returns *which* part of a formal statement changed the claim, *in
+which direction*, with a *checked* countermodel. In particular, none does so when both statements are
+true theorems, where whole-statement equivalence is trivial.
+
+## 2. Definitions (Lean: `ClaimDelta.lean`, `DriftSoundness.lean`)
+
+- **Interpretation space.** `T` is the type of the operators shared by `R` and `L` that lie outside
+  linear arithmetic and logic: `^`, `*` between variables, `gcd`, `List.length`, `List.reverse`, and
+  so on. Abstraction is **per occurrence** (interpreted uses such as `2 * n` stay concrete).
+- **Uniform entailment and countermodel.**
+  - `SchemaEntails P Q := ∀ x : T, P x → Q x`.
+  - `Separates P Q x := Q x ∧ ¬ P x`.
+  - `StrictlyWeaker Q P := SchemaEntails P Q ∧ ∃ x, Separates P Q x`.
+- **Edits and chain.** Edits are the maximal mismatching subterms of the two elaborated terms. The
+  traversal enters binders with equal types, splits implications into hypothesis and conclusion, and
+  enters an application only when exactly one explicit argument differs. The chain is
+  `S₀ = R, …, Sₙ = L`, with every `Sₖ` abstracted over the same `T`.
+- **Law policy.** Concrete rewriting with a fixed list of equational laws (commutativity, `gcd_comm`,
+  `pow_two`, `length_append`, …). Every law tactic ends by *using the other statement* (`simpa … using h`,
+  `exact h`, `(h x).symm`), so a law certificate is never a separate proof of the target.
+
+## 3. Theorems (all checked by Lean; fresh kernel replay exit 0, LOCAL)
+
+| Theorem | Statement | Axioms |
+|---|---|---|
+| `monotone_strict` | `A ⇒ B` (uniform), `C` strictly weaker than `B`, `C ⇒ D` ⊢ `D` strictly weaker than `A`, **and every countermodel of the step (B, C) is a countermodel of (A, D)** | none |
+| `localize_weaker` / `localize_stronger` | the same, with the other edits schema-equivalent | none |
+| `harmless_edit` | a schema-equivalent edit can be undone without changing any relation to the reference | none |
+| `chain_entails_last` | a chain of uniform entailments composes | none |
+| `equiv_symm`, `equiv_trans` | — | none |
+| `cancellation` | **counterexample**: `x ≤ 1 → x ≤ 2` weakens, `x ≤ 2 → x ≤ 2 ∧ x ≠ 2` strengthens, and the end points are equivalent. Per-edit verdicts in mixed directions do **not** compose. | standard (`omega`) |
+| v0.8 `ClaimDelta.lean` | soundness of separation, asymmetry, refinement, and the policy counterexamples | none |
+
+**Candidate paper theorem (Localization).** Let `R` and `L` be elaborated statements with edit chain
+`S₀ … Sₙ` over a common abstraction. Suppose every edit is certified uniformly forward-entailing and
+edit `k` carries a countermodel `x`. Then `L` is strictly weaker than `R` as a claim about the shared
+operators, and `x` witnesses this globally. Under the policy, the explanation "edit `k` weakens the
+claim, as `x` shows" is therefore a kernel-checked fact about the whole pair, not a heuristic. Without
+the one-direction hypothesis no such statement holds (`cancellation`).
+
+## 4. Results
+
+### Development suite `cmcd_v09_dev` (21 cases: ℕ 9, logic 5, ℤ 4, lists 3; labels hashed before freeze)
+
+| Run | Verdict exact | Localization exact | Wrong decisive | Inconclusive |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Block 1, frozen, first run (LOCAL) | 16/21 | — | **1** | 4 |
+| Block 2, after fixes; now development data (LOCAL) | 21/21 | 21/21 | 0 | 0 |
+
+**Block 1 failure analysis.**
+- **`n06` (wrong).** `gcd a b → gcd b a` was split into two argument edits, creating the meaningless
+  intermediate `gcd b b`, and the global law check did not exist yet. Result: a **false mismatch**.
+  Fix: an application with more than one differing explicit argument is now a single edit, and a
+  global antecedent-using law check was added.
+- **`l03`.** The same splitting problem (`x = 1` against `1 = x`).
+- **`l02`, `li03`, `z03`.** Witness gaps: no constant-4 function, no list or negative-integer
+  instances, and no closer for a uniqueness refutation.
+
+### Adversarial suite `cmcd_v09_adversarial` (6 cases, block 2, single run, LOCAL)
+
+- **False equivalence certificates: 0 of 5 eligible.**
+- **Label match 4/6.** Both mismatches are **errors in the author's labels**, not engine errors:
+  - `a1`: `n < 2^n` does imply `1 ≤ 2^n` for every interpretation of `^` (at `n = 0`, `0 < f 0`).
+    The Lean-checked verdict CLAIM_LEAN_WEAKER is right.
+  - `a6`: `2^n < n` is unsatisfiable at `n = 0` under every interpretation, so the true relation is
+    LEAN_STRONGER. The engine said INCONCLUSIVE, not a wrong verdict.
+
+  The labels are kept as written. This is direct evidence that same-author labels are unreliable, and
+  it is why the blind suite must be written by someone else (`benchmark/cmcd_blind/README.md`).
+
+### Domains
+
+ℕ arithmetic, ℤ arithmetic, lists (`length`, `++`, `reverse`), and quantifier logic (`∀∃` vs `∃∀`,
+`∃!` vs `∃`, `∀` vs `∃`) all run through one principle: shared-operator abstraction plus a certified
+chain, with type-indexed witness pools. **ℝ, sets, groups and topology need Mathlib: PENDING.**
+
+## 5. Gates
+
+| Gate | Status |
+|---|---|
+| 1 Infrastructure | Lean pinning done; GitHub verification **PENDING** |
+| 2 Soundness | met for the drift relation (Lean, replayed) |
+| 3 Generality (≥3 domains) | met in core Lean (4 domains); Mathlib domains not met |
+| 4 Adversarial | 0 false certificates on 6 cases (small) |
+| 5 Independent evaluation | **not met**: blind suite and ProofNetVerif PENDING |
+| 6 Novelty | localization with certified countermodels appears absent from SemantEq, ACE, BEq+ and BPF as published; **not established** until a wider search and expert review |
+| 7 Release | not met |
+
+## 6. Limits
+
+- **Core Lean only**, which is the decisive gap for real research statements.
+- **Policy-relative.** Verdicts depend on which operators are interpreted, and the law list is fixed.
+- **Search-limited.** Witness pools and tactic portfolios are finite: an INCONCLUSIVE verdict means
+  "not found", not "equivalent".
+- **Order-dependent.** Edits are applied in traversal order. Localization is only claimed when every
+  edit points in one direction.
+- **Same author, small suites.** All results are on author-written suites (21 + 6 cases).
